A||||12||loafer87gt||What did the world trace center mean to you?||||3||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||09-16-2001||06:08 PM||||I am doing a presentation this Tuesday for one of my Art History classes on the subversion of inteded meanings/functions in architecture. For example, it is unlikely that the architect of the WTC intended for the building to be an icon for capitalism and the American dream. I thought it would be interesting to poll some of the forum members and ask them what the building meant to them, and why they felt this way. Thanks in advance for your participation<P>Edit: Thats TRADE center. My fingers got carried away a little bit while I was typing<p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||09-16-2001||06:27 PM||||Being from Chicago. Ive been to the world trade center .Until Tuesday I always they were very boring as far as tall buildings go just a couple of cheap squre tubes no personality.I now have a different view of them.||||reg||9
Z||000002||maryjane||09-16-2001||06:48 PM||||I never gave it much thought before you asked, New Yorkers could better answer this question. I assume you meant what did it mean "before". I'll give it a shot tho.<BR>It stood for what this country does best, and what I hope it's always remembered for.<BR>Not our war making ability, not our arrogance. We build, and we do it on a grand scale. We overcome adversity, thru the back-breaking labor of our people and the sure and steady progress of the designers, the skilled artisans and the architects. A symbol of our faith in the economy, the American dollar, and the future of this country.  We build things like the Panama Canal, the Space Shuttle, and the WTC, not because we have to, but simply because we can. It was our Tower of Pisa, our Pyramid.<BR>It is what we are. A monument to our abilities and resolve.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||09-16-2001||07:10 PM||||Simplicity can be the most beautiful. Like said, look at Pyramids and Washington Monument. Untill last Tuesday though, I have to admit I didnt really think about it, I dont see the icon thing, it was just a couple of buildings. It now has become a lot more.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||09-16-2001||08:34 PM||||The old adage, "Too many eggs in one basket comes to mind" <BR>In Vietnam the words "Spread it out, One round will get you all." was uttered many times"<BR>Maybe a lot of our systems of business and government should be less centralized and less obvious targets.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rick 88||09-16-2001||08:44 PM||||It was a place I always wanted to visit. A city within a city.....<P>Rick 88||||reg||9
Z||000006||starfighter007@msn.com||09-16-2001||08:45 PM||||I miss those guys now . I dont know why but I am like mesmorized by what happened to them.Its like I cant stop watching the planes hit them and when they went down I just still cant believe my eyes.It is still so sureal to me.I hope and pray that they rebuild them again even if nobody would work there anymore .I truly miss them fellas. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000007||Raydar||09-16-2001||08:48 PM||||I agree with Wolfhound. <BR>And, firefighters <I>hate</I> skyscrapers.<BR>The only reason they build them so tall is the shortage of land in most major cities. In order to accommodate the largest number of people they have to build "up", because they can't build "out".<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||California Kid||09-16-2001||11:52 PM||||Can't say that I've ever given the Twin Tower buildings much thought, other than if a big earthquake hit the area, unbelievable life lose. Could you imagine if those towers had fallen over, instead of vertical colaspe??<P>So far I've lost two co-workers there and one in the plane crashes. As the days unfold, countless families around the world will be impacted by the loss of friends, and loved ones.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||09-17-2001||11:05 AM||||Great stuff guys, thank you very much. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||09-17-2001||02:29 PM||||I went to Manhattan with my grandparents about 10 years ago.  I didn't go in or up the World Trade Towers, but I did look up at them from the streets below.  From the top of the Empire State Building, the Towers made the Statue of Liberty look so small which was odd because I had thought the Statue would be much bigger than what it is.  I thought the same thing about the possibility of a big earthquake and what would happen but never anything like this.<P>I remember the very first time I saw the Trade Towers for real.  It was on a train at the north end of Manhattan, just before going into the underground subway.  I can remember the Japanese tourists in the seats next to me pointing and saying "Look, Twin Towas!...  Twin Towas!  Ooooohhh!"  Their reactions were kind of funny at the time, however, seeing them tower Manhattan was really an amazing sight.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 09-17-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000011||Fierowrecker||09-17-2001||11:34 PM||||Hey Loafer!<BR>Please read ALL of what I say...<BR>Otherwise you may get the wrong impression...<BR>The World Trade Center, a pair of tall buildings...<BR>They did not mean a thing to me, even though I knew they had suffered a bomb blast, but like most American structures, they had survived, who cares...<BR>BUT, having been destroyed in an organized terrorist attack, and killing thousands of people, now the absence of those buildings are the focus of the American people...<BR>In the past, rallying cries of "Remember the Main!" or "Remember the Alamo!" or even "Remember Pearl Harbour!" brought strong emotions to the American people.<BR>But now, the World Trade Center, and the Pentagon, and all who lost their lives to the cowardly acts of terrorists will bring strong emotions to ALL the people of America...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cheever3000||09-17-2001||11:52 PM||||I have (had?) no attachment to the WTC towers, and I never liked New York City.  I was there once briefly in my early teens, so maybe that's not a fair chance.  But the skyline <B>IS</B> New York to me, and New York <B>IS</B> America.  And, by golly, you just don't attack New York!  <P>I didn't have to see it all week on TV to know that those people would come together like they have.  They're <I>Americans</I>, and any of us would do the same (remember Oklahoma City).  And I just want to say, <B>I love New York!</B>  And I love my country, and I'll stand with them any day to support and defend our home.<BR>||||reg||1
